A||||4||David DeVoe||2.8 Damper repair sleeve||||5||
Z||000000||David DeVoe||12-18-2001||06:54 PM||||Just tried to put a repair sleeve on my damper...it won't fit. My damper has a very slight groove from the seal and does'nt appear badly worn. Anybody have any input on this. I'm told the sleeve should start on the hub 1/4 in. at least before driving it all the way on. Perhaps I don't even need it.<BR>Dave||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||12-18-2001||08:05 PM||||sounds like you've got the wrong sleeve.<P>Well... if you put it back and it leaks then you'll know you need the sleeve.<P>Often tho... if they are worn enough to need the sleeve the rubber in the damper may be on it's way out. check it over very carefully. If it's been oil soaked or looks dry rotted cracked etc... replace the whole damper. It's relatively cheap insurance.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||jelly2m8||12-18-2001||08:29 PM||||ogre's right about checking the dampner ( harmonic balancer) for oil contamination in the rubber.<P> The repair sleeves only go on one way. At one end it is tapered in, to allow the balancer to enter the oil seal in the timing cover properly. This end is the end that you tap on to install the sleeve.<P> The repair sleeves are a very tight fit. You won't be able to slip it 1/4" on by hand. Usually they only go on a little better than 1/16" before you have to 'force' them on.<P> If you do use a repair sleeve, take emery paper to the hub and clean it up before hand.<P> Use some red (preferably) Loctite on the hub before you install the sleeve.<P> Lay the harmonic balancer face down on a sturdy surface. Once you start the sleeve, you should use a block of wood on the end of the sleeve and tap it down gently and evenly. <P> You will notice that the sleeve will swell a small amount as it goes over the end of the hub.<P> The trick is to go slow and easy with it.<P>best of luck!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||David DeVoe||12-18-2001||08:30 PM||||Ogre<BR>I've checked the damper over real good and it looks fine except for the very slight groove. The rubber is good and the whole thing is clean and straight. Thanks once again for your input.<BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000004||GTDude||12-19-2001||09:16 AM||||There is also a slightly offset seal available for this problem.  I believe NAPA carries them.  Good Luck<P>phil pettus<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DSCF0003a_3.JPG"> <P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
